## What is different for citizens

The rules most buyers read about, the schemes and the USD 375,000 threshold, are there because the Non Citizens (Property Restriction) Act limits what foreigners can buy. They do not limit you as a citizen: you can buy a house, an apartment or land on the open market, like anyone living here.

You can also buy inside the schemes. The Economic Development Board's guidelines say a citizen may buy a home under the IRS, RES or PDS and pay in **rupees or any freely convertible foreign currency**.

## Serviced plots in PDS projects

Citizens and members of the diaspora registered under the Mauritian Diaspora Scheme may buy a serviced plot of up to 2,100 m² in a PDS project and have **ten years** to build their house. Foreigners have five years and can buy only one plot.

## The Mauritian Diaspora Scheme

The Mauritian Diaspora Scheme is a government initiative to bring Mauritians abroad back home to take part in the economy. You apply to the EDB for a Mauritian Diaspora Certificate under one of three categories: professional, self employed or young professional.

Apply either **before you return to Mauritius**, or **no more than 180 days** after you start your first job or register your first business here. The EDB's guidelines and the regulations are linked below.

## Buying from abroad in practice

- A notary handles the sale; you can give someone you trust a power of attorney if you cannot be here to sign.
- Ask for every cost in writing before you commit.
- If you want help finding a home on the west coast, tell us what you are looking for.

*Rules and amounts change. Everything here was checked against the Economic Development Board's own pages on 2 October 2026; confirm the details with the EDB, your notary and the developer before you sign.*
